steps.body:
Every `new`, and every time a `std::vector` or a `std::string` grows, ends in a call to `malloc`. It searches its free lists, may take a lock, and now and then asks the operating system for more memory: usually tens of nanoseconds, sometimes far more, and you can't tell in advance which. A market-data handler has a few microseconds per message, so its **hot path**, the code that runs for every message, should allocate nothing at all. The only way to know it doesn't is to count.

The lessons on errors in fast code, on string_view, and on polymorphism costs borrowed a counting `operator new` to prove a point. Here is how it works, and where it can mislead you. C++ lets a program replace the global `operator new`, the function that every `new` expression and every standard container's default allocator call for memory. This is the counter the course uses, defined once outside any function:

```cpp harness
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <new>
#include <vector>

{{code}}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> prices(100);
    std::cout << g_allocs << " allocs, " << g_bytes << " bytes\n";
}
```

```cpp
long g_allocs = 0;  // calls to operator new
long g_bytes = 0;   // bytes they asked for

void* operator new(std::size_t n) {
    ++g_allocs;
    g_bytes += n;
    if (void* p = std::malloc(n)) {
        return p;
    }
    throw std::bad_alloc();
}

void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
```

- It takes memory from `std::malloc`, since a `new` inside it would call itself forever, and throws `std::bad_alloc` when there's none, as the real one does. The `operator delete`s give it back with `std::free`. The compiler calls the second, sized one when it knows the size.
- `new T[n]` goes through `operator new[]`, whose default calls this one, so arrays count too. Types with an `alignas` above 16 use a separate aligned form, which this lesson's part on arenas adds.
- Read the counters before and after some code: the difference is what that code allocated. Start after reading input, since in libstdc++ every `std::cin >> x` into a `double` allocates once (57 bytes here), while an `int` doesn't.
- The counter sees every thread's allocations, and with several threads its plain `long`s would race: they'd need `std::atomic<long>` (the lesson on atomics). To count only a hot thread's, the lesson on low-latency design adds a `thread_local` flag.

The Stack and Heap lesson showed a vector doubling its capacity as it grows. Now you can see what that costs.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <new>
#include <vector>

long g_allocs = 0;  // calls to operator new
long g_bytes = 0;   // bytes they asked for

void* operator new(std::size_t n) {
    ++g_allocs;
    g_bytes += n;
    if (void* p = std::malloc(n)) {
        return p;
    }
    throw std::bad_alloc();
}

void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

int main() {
    long allocs = g_allocs;
    long bytes = g_bytes;
    std::vector<Tick> grown;
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        grown.push_back({i, 100.0, 100.25, 5, 7});
    }
    std::cout << "grown: " << g_allocs - allocs << " allocs, " << g_bytes - bytes << " bytes\n";

    allocs = g_allocs;
    bytes = g_bytes;
    std::vector<Tick> reserved;
    reserved.reserve(1000);
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        reserved.push_back({i, 100.0, 100.25, 5, 7});
    }
    std::cout << "reserved: " << g_allocs - allocs << " allocs, " << g_bytes - bytes << " bytes\n";

    allocs = g_allocs;
    std::vector<Tick> batch;  // one buffer for every batch of the day
    batch.reserve(1000);
    for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) {
        batch.clear();        // drops the ticks, keeps the capacity
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
            batch.push_back({i, 100.0, 100.25, 5, 7});
        }
    }
    std::cout << "3 batches in one reserved vector: " << g_allocs - allocs << " allocs\n";
    return 0;
}

