steps.body:
Some hot-path work needs a few short-lived containers per message or per batch: the ticks of one snapshot, the depth at each price. An **arena** hands out memory by moving a pointer forward through a buffer, frees nothing one piece at a time, and drops everything at once when it's done. C++17 has one in `<memory_resource>`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;
    double bid, ask;
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    ticks.push_back({1, 100.0, 100.25, 3, 4});
    depth[10000] += 3;
    std::cout << ticks.size() << ' ' << depth.size() << '\n';
}
```

```cpp
std::array<std::byte, 64 * 1024> buffer;  // the arena's memory, on the stack
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena(buffer.data(), buffer.size());
std::pmr::vector<Tick> ticks(&arena);     // a vector that allocates from the arena
std::pmr::map<int, double> depth(&arena);
```

- `std::byte`, from `<cstddef>`, is C++17's type for raw memory: a byte with no arithmetic.
- `std::pmr::vector<T>`, `std::pmr::map<K, V>`, and `std::pmr::string` are the standard containers with a *polymorphic allocator*: give one a memory resource, and every allocation it makes, nodes included, comes from there.
- Freeing does nothing. The memory comes back only when the arena is destroyed or `release()`d, so make one arena per batch, and reserve, since each growth of a vector leaves its old buffer behind as dead space.
- When the buffer runs out, the arena quietly asks its *upstream* resource for more, the heap by default. Pass `std::pmr::null_memory_resource()` as a third argument, and it throws `std::bad_alloc` instead, a good guard in tests.
- `std::pmr::unsynchronized_pool_resource` is the pool version, for one thread: it keeps freed blocks by size and hands them out again.

The default upstream allocates through the aligned form, `operator new(std::size_t, std::align_val_t)`, the one `new` also uses for types with an `alignas` above 16. A counter that replaces only the plain form never sees those calls, so the counter here replaces the aligned pair too, taking memory from `std::aligned_alloc`, which wants a size that's a multiple of the alignment.

example.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <memory_resource>
#include <new>
#include <vector>

long g_allocs = 0;  // calls to operator new
long g_bytes = 0;   // bytes they asked for

void* operator new(std::size_t n) {
    ++g_allocs;
    g_bytes += n;
    if (void* p = std::malloc(n)) {
        return p;
    }
    throw std::bad_alloc();
}

void* operator new(std::size_t n, std::align_val_t align) {  // std::pmr asks through this one
    ++g_allocs;
    g_bytes += n;
    std::size_t a = static_cast<std::size_t>(align);
    if (void* p = std::aligned_alloc(a, (n + a - 1) / a * a)) {  // a size that's a multiple of a
        return p;
    }
    throw std::bad_alloc();
}

void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::align_val_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t, std::align_val_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

int main() {
    std::array<std::byte, 64 * 1024> buffer;  // 64 KB on the stack, reused by every batch
    long allocs = g_allocs;
    double depth_total = 0;
    for (int batch = 0; batch < 3; ++batch) {
        std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena(buffer.data(), buffer.size());
        std::pmr::vector<Tick> ticks(&arena);
        ticks.reserve(1000);
        std::pmr::map<int, double> depth(&arena);  // bid size at each price, in cents
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
            ticks.push_back({i, 100.0 + i % 4 * 0.25, 100.25 + i % 4 * 0.25, 1.0 + batch, 2.0});
            depth[10000 + i % 4 * 25] += ticks.back().bid_sz;
        }
        depth_total += depth[10025];
    }  // each arena gives the whole buffer back at once
    std::cout << "3 batches in the arena: " << g_allocs - allocs << " allocs, depth at 100.25: " << depth_total << '\n';

    allocs = g_allocs;
    {
        std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena(buffer.data(), buffer.size());
        std::pmr::vector<Tick> ticks(&arena);  // no reserve: each growth leaves a dead buffer behind
        for (int i = 0; i < 2000; ++i) {
            ticks.push_back({i, 100.0, 100.25, 1.0, 2.0});
        }
    }
    std::cout << "2000 ticks, no reserve: " << g_allocs - allocs << " allocs from the heap\n";

    std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource strict(buffer.data(), buffer.size(), std::pmr::null_memory_resource());
    try {
        std::pmr::vector<Tick> ticks(2000, Tick{}, &strict);  // 80,000 bytes don't fit in 64 KB
    } catch (const std::bad_alloc&) {
